# Normalise line endings in the repository.
#
# Without this, a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true checks these files
# out as CRLF while the generators that produce some of them write LF -- so
# `vscode-config.js --check` compared a CRLF file against LF output and
# failed in CI on Windows only, for a reason that had nothing to do with the
# content.
#
# LF everywhere, including on Windows: every consumer here (Node, git, VS
# Code, PowerShell) reads LF fine, and it removes a whole class of "works on
# my machine" diffs.
* text=auto eol=lf

# VBS is the exception. Windows Script Host requires CRLF, and the launcher
# this project generates is written with explicit \r\n for that reason. A
# checkout that converts it to LF produces a launcher Windows will not run.
*.vbs text eol=crlf

# Binaries, left exactly as they are.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.node binary
*.key binary

# Test fixtures whose bytes are the point of the test. Any transformation
# here would change what is being asserted.
src/test/fixtures/** -text
